Despite these advances, transferring semantic and visual dynamics knowledge to humanoid whole-body control presents two challenges.
\textbf{\ding{182} Insufficient Whole-Body Motion Priors.}
Compared with fixed-base manipulation, humanoid control involves higher-dimensional motion with strong inter-joint and temporal dependencies. Existing systems initialize their backbones from vision--language, video-dynamics, or world--action pretraining~\cite{grootn1,psi0,motionwam,wholebodywam_wbc}, which does not itself equip action generators with whole-body motion priors aligned with the target humanoid's kinematic space. Action experts must therefore learn these motion regularities during policy adaptation, often from limited humanoid demonstrations.
\textbf{\ding{183} Insufficient Differentiation of Motion Components.}
Root motion, body articulation, and hand control differ in scale and precision requirements. Unified generators model these heterogeneous components within a shared prediction process~\cite{openhlm,motionwam}, potentially creating competition among learning objectives. Hybrid designs separate upper-body joint targets from lower-body locomotion commands~\cite{humanoidexo,wholebodyvla}, but this execution-level split does not itself provide dedicated root, body, and hand generators. Both designs thus place substantial demands on the action predictor to accommodate component-specific requirements while maintaining whole-body coordination.


% 逻辑 
% 1. 提出并概述方法, 对应图 1 中部
% 2. 回应问题1，强动作先验: 选择 T2M 骨干，对应图 2(c)
% 3. 回应问题2, 强: 3阶段框架, 对应图 2(f)
% 4. 概述取得的效果, 对应图 1 顶部

Recent text-to-motion (T2M) methods learn structured motion distributions conditioned on text or spatial constraints~\cite{temos,mdm,mld,t2mgpt,momask,omnicontrol}, providing inter-joint and temporal priors for humanoid action generation. 
Building on these advances, we present \textbf{Kimodo-Policy}, a humanoid VLA model combining a pretrained T2M backbone with a three-stage root--body--hand architecture.
\textbf{\ding{182} Strong Whole-Body Motion Priors.}
We transfer Kimodo's pretrained motion generator into the VLA framework (Fig.~\ref{fig:policy_routes}(c)), equipping action generation with priors over inter-joint coordination and temporal structure~\cite{kimodo}.
These priors support high-dimensional action prediction and allow VLA training to focus on grounding established motion capabilities in visual observations and task instructions, easing the burden of learning motion regularities from limited humanoid demonstrations.
\textbf{\ding{183} Strong Component-Specific Motion Modeling.}
Building on these priors, our three-stage architecture uses dedicated generators to accommodate distinct control requirements (Fig.~\ref{fig:policy_routes}(f)). The root stage predicts translation and heading; the body stage generates articulated motion conditioned on root trajectories; and the hand stage predicts controls from future body features and visual observations. These cross-stage dependencies support component specialization and whole-body coordination.
We pretraining on diverse humanoid demonstrations enables Kimodo-Policy to generate coordinated whole-body actions conditioned on visual observations, language instructions, and motion history. With 678M total and only 92M trainable parameters, it achieves strong performance on HumanoidArena~\cite{humanoidarena}, SIMPLE~\cite{simple}, and real-world loco-manipulation tasks. Scaling studies show consistent gains from more VLA pretraining data, larger models, and stronger T2M priors. Our main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item We introduce \textbf{Kimodo-Policy}, a humanoid VLA framework that transfers pretrained T2M motion priors to visually guided whole-body control. VLA pretraining on diverse humanoid demonstrations grounds these priors in visual observations, language instructions, and motion history for closed-loop execution.

  \item We propose a three-stage root--body--hand architecture with dedicated generators that accommodate distinct control requirements. Explicit cross-stage conditioning preserves dependencies among motion components, supporting specialized generation and whole-body coordination.
  
  \item We demonstrate strong performance on HumanoidArena, SIMPLE, and real-world humanoid tasks with 678M total and only 92M trainable parameters. Scaling studies show consistent gains from larger VLA pretraining datasets, greater model capacity, and stronger pretrained T2M priors.
\end{itemize}
